---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Bowflex Reviews (2026): Treadmills, Max Trainers and Ownership";
const description =
  "Bowflex now sells three folding treadmills, the T6, T9 and T16, alongside its Max Trainer line. Who owns it now, how the range lines up, and how the M5 and M7 compare.";

const LINEUP = [
  {
    id: "bowflex-max-trainer-m7",
    name: "Bowflex Max Trainer M7",
    tier: "Flagship Cardio",
    price: "$1,599-$1,799",
    type: "Elliptical-Stepper Hybrid",
    detail: "14 resistance levels",
    summary:
      "Bowflex's top Max Trainer, with 14 resistance levels, a larger backlit console, and the deepest JRNY app integration in the lineup. Not a treadmill.",
    href: "/bowflex-max-trainer-m7/",
  },
  {
    id: "bowflex-max-trainer-m5",
    name: "Bowflex Max Trainer M5",
    tier: "Entry Cardio",
    price: "$999-$1,199",
    type: "Elliptical-Stepper Hybrid",
    detail: "8 resistance levels",
    summary:
      "The budget entry point into the Max Trainer line, sharing the same frame and motion as the M7 with a simpler console and fewer resistance levels. Also not a treadmill.",
    href: "/bowflex-max-trainer-m5/",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Bowflex", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/bowflex" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does Bowflex make treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Bowflex currently sells three folding treadmills: the T6, the T9 and the T16. None of them needs a subscription to work, and each can connect to the JRNY app and to other training apps, which carry their own subscriptions. Bowflex is also known for the Max Trainer, a low-impact stepper-elliptical hybrid that is not a treadmill. Our Bowflex T6 review covers the entry model in detail.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Who owns Bowflex?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Johnson Health Tech, which also owns Horizon Fitness and Matrix Fitness. Bowflex was previously the flagship brand of Nautilus Inc, which renamed itself BowFlex Inc in 2023, filed for Chapter 11 in March 2024 and sold the BowFlex, Schwinn and JRNY brands to Johnson Health Tech in a sale that closed in April 2024.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the JRNY app?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "JRNY is Bowflex's coaching and workout app, offering guided, auto-adjusting workouts, progress tracking, and goal-setting features across compatible Bowflex equipment including the Max Trainer line. It's conceptually similar to iFit on NordicTrack treadmills, and it's an optional subscription rather than a requirement for using the equipment.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which Bowflex Max Trainer should I buy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Max Trainer M7 is the better choice for anyone who expects to progress into high-intensity training, thanks to its 14 resistance levels and deeper JRNY integration. The Max Trainer M5 is the smarter starting point for beginners or budget-conscious buyers who want to try the format before spending more.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a Bowflex Max Trainer a good alternative to a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends entirely on your goal. If you want lower-impact cardio and don't need to run, the Max Trainer line is a legitimate and in some ways superior alternative. If running specifically is your goal, no Max Trainer can replace a treadmill, and you should look at an actual treadmill instead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does Bowflex still make home gyms and strength equipment?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Bowflex's original and still-current core business is home strength equipment, including its signature resistance-rod home gym machines and adjustable dumbbells. The Max Trainer cardio line is a newer addition to that lineup rather than a replacement for it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-06-17",
      dateModified: "2026-10-08",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-06-17",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-08",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: ["bowflex", "bowflex reviews", "bowflex max trainer", "does bowflex make treadmills", "jrny app"],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Max Trainer Lineup", link: "#lineup" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bowflex" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bowflex)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6"><TrustBadge /></div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">Brand Guide</span>
          <span class="bg-amber-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">No Treadmills in This Lineup</span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">Updated June 2026</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Bowflex</span> Reviews
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Bowflex built its name on home strength equipment, expanded into
          cardio with the Max Trainer, and now sells three folding treadmills.
          Here's the honest breakdown.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#lineup" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">See the Max Trainer Lineup</a>
          <a href="#does-bowflex-make-treadmills" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">Does Bowflex Make Treadmills?</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 8, 2026</strong></p>
        <div class="ml-auto"><SocialProof users="6.4k" /></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
            <a href="#does-bowflex-make-treadmills" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Does Bowflex Make Treadmills?</a>
            <a href="#brand-history" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. About Bowflex</a>
            <a href="#lineup" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. The Bowflex Max Trainer Lineup</a>
            <a href="#build-quality" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Build Quality and How the Max Trainer Works</a>
            <a href="#app" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. The JRNY App Experience</a>
            <a href="#vs-competitors" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Max Trainer vs. a Treadmill</a>
            <a href="#specific-needs" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Bowflex for Specific Situations</a>
            <a href="#which-one" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Which Bowflex Max Trainer Should You Buy?</a>
            <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Warranty and Customer Support</a>
            <a href="#complaints" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Common Owner Complaints (and How to Avoid Them)</a>
            <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A meaningful share of readers land on this page expecting a
          Bowflex treadmill guide, and we want to answer that honestly in
          the first few sentences rather than burying it: yes, it does. Bowflex
          sells three folding treadmills — the T6, T9 and T16 — alongside its
          home strength equipment and a genuinely capable low-impact cardio
          line called the Max Trainer. This guide covers how the range fits
          together, who owns the brand now, and the Max Trainer in depth.
        </p>

        <div id="does-bowflex-make-treadmills" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Does Bowflex Make Treadmills?</h2>
        <p>
          Yes. This page used to say otherwise, and it was out of date: Bowflex
          currently sells three folding treadmills, all marketed as working
          without a subscription. On Bowflex's own listings, checked in October
          2026, the range looks like this:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bowflex T6</strong> — the entry model: a 20 x 60 inch running
          area, 0–15% motorised incline, 0.5–12 mph, a 325 lb maximum user weight
          and an LCD console rather than a touchscreen. Our
          <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/">Bowflex T6 review</a> covers it in detail,
          including how it compares with the Sole F63.</li>
          <li><strong>Bowflex T9</strong> — the step up: a wider 22 x 60 inch running
          area, the same 0–15% incline and 12 mph top speed, a 350 lb maximum user
          weight and a larger colour display.</li>
          <li><strong>Bowflex T16</strong> — the top of the range, positioned around
          entertainment and a bigger screen.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Bowflex lists a lifetime frame and motor warranty on the T6 and T9, with
          three years on mechanical parts and one year on labour. Each treadmill
          connects over Bluetooth to Bowflex's JRNY app and to other training apps,
          which carry their own subscriptions; the treadmills themselves run
          without one. Prices move constantly, so compare on the day — our guide to
          the <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/">best time of year to buy a treadmill</a>
          explains when the real discounts tend to appear.
        </p>
        <p>
          The confusion is understandable. For most of its history Bowflex was a
          strength brand first, and its best-known cardio product, the Max
          Trainer, is not a treadmill at all — it is a compact, low-impact
          stepper-elliptical hybrid. If you want to run, look at the treadmills
          above or our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-for-home-reviews/">Best Treadmills for Home</a>
          and <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/">Best Folding Treadmills</a>
          guides. If you want lower-impact cardio in a smaller footprint, keep
          reading: the Max Trainer line is covered in full below.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brand-history" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. About Bowflex</h2>
        <p>
          Bowflex is now owned by Johnson Health Tech, the company behind
          Horizon Fitness and Matrix Fitness. Johnson Health Tech bought the
          BowFlex, Schwinn and JRNY brands in April 2024, after BowFlex Inc —
          the company previously called Nautilus Inc — filed for Chapter 11.
          Bowflex itself built its reputation starting in the late 1980s around its
          signature resistance-rod home gym machines — the all-in-one
          strength systems that made "Bowflex" practically synonymous with
          home strength training for an entire generation of American
          households, largely through direct-response television marketing
          that turned the brand into a household name well beyond the
          fitness-equipment industry itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          For most of its history, Bowflex's product strategy stayed
          centered on strength: the home gym systems, and later adjustable
          dumbbells and other resistance equipment. The Max Trainer line,
          introduced as the brand's push into cardio, represents a genuine
          expansion beyond that original identity rather than an
          incremental addition — it's a completely different exercise
          category, low-impact interval cardio, built to complement rather
          than replace the strength equipment the brand is still best known
          for.
        </p>
        <p>
          Alongside the Max Trainer hardware, Bowflex has built out JRNY, a
          coaching and workout app that spans multiple pieces of Bowflex
          equipment, not just the Max Trainer line. JRNY represents the
          same broader industry shift toward connected, subscription-based
          coaching content that NordicTrack's iFit represents for
          treadmills — a sign that even a brand without a treadmill in its
          lineup is competing in the same connected-fitness landscape as
          the brands that do make them.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div><p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">2</p><p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Max Trainer Models</p></div>
            <div><p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">0</p><p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Treadmills in Lineup</p></div>
            <div><p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">$999+</p><p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Starting Price</p></div>
            <div><p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">1986</p><p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Brand Founded</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Bowflex Max Trainer Lineup</h2>
        <p>
          Bowflex's cardio offering is currently anchored by two models in
          the Max Trainer line. Neither is a treadmill — both use a
          combined elliptical-and-stepper motion against adjustable
          resistance rather than a running belt.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 my-10">
        {LINEUP.map((model) => (
          <div id={model.id} class="scroll-mt-24 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
            <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-white font-black text-lg">{model.name}</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{model.tier} Tier</p>
              </div>
              <span class="bg-[#5AA9FF]/20 text-[#5AA9FF] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">{model.price}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6">
              <div class="flex gap-6 mb-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
                <span><strong class="text-gray-900">{model.type}</strong></span>
                <span><strong class="text-gray-900">{model.detail}</strong></span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">{model.summary}</p>
              <a href={model.href} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] font-bold no-underline">
                Read the full review
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m9 18 6-6-6-6" /></svg>
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="lineup-detail" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">How the M5 and M7 Actually Differ in Practice</h2>
        <p>
          The two models share the same underlying frame and the same
          combined elliptical-stepper motion, which means the core exercise
          experience feels nearly identical between them. Where they
          genuinely diverge is resistance range and console sophistication:
          the M7's 14 resistance levels give considerably more headroom for
          an intermediate or advanced user to keep progressing over months
          and years, while the M5's 8 levels are plenty for a beginner but
          can start to feel limiting once you've built real conditioning.
          The M7's larger backlit console and deeper JRNY integration are
          the other meaningful differences, translating to a more
          data-rich, guided workout experience for the roughly $500-$600
          price premium.
        </p>
        <p>
          Testers who tried both back to back generally agreed that the
          right choice comes down to how confident you are about your own
          training trajectory rather than which machine is objectively
          "better." Someone brand new to interval training who isn't sure
          the format will stick is better served starting with the M5 and
          upgrading later if needed; someone who already trains seriously
          and knows they'll want more resistance headroom is better served
          buying the M7 once, rather than upgrading twice.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="build-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Build Quality and How the Max Trainer Works</h2>
        <p>
          Both Max Trainer models use the same core mechanism: pedals
          connected to a combined elliptical-glide-and-vertical-stepper
          motion, resisted by an electromagnetic resistance system rather
          than mechanical friction. That electromagnetic design is worth
          understanding on its own, since it's the reason resistance
          changes happen instantly and silently mid-workout — there's no
          physical brake pad or belt tension to adjust, just a magnetic
          field strength the console controls electronically.
        </p>
        <p>
          The handlebars move in sync with the pedal motion across both
          models, engaging your arms and upper body alongside your legs in
          a way a treadmill workout simply doesn't. That full-body
          engagement is a real part of why testers consistently reported
          higher perceived effort per minute on the Max Trainer than on an
          equivalent-duration treadmill session — you're working more
          muscle groups simultaneously, which raises heart rate faster for
          a given amount of time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both machines fold upright using a simple latch mechanism,
          collapsing to a meaningfully smaller footprint than any
          treadmill in our reviews, including compact folding models. This
          is one of the Max Trainer line's clearest practical advantages
          for apartment dwellers or anyone without a dedicated permanent
          workout space.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="app" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The JRNY App Experience</h2>
        <p>
          JRNY functions as Bowflex's answer to iFit — a coaching app
          offering guided, auto-adjusting workouts, progress tracking, and
          goal-setting across compatible equipment including both Max
          Trainer models. It's an optional subscription rather than a
          requirement: both the M5 and M7 run their built-in Max Interval
          programs fully without ever connecting to the app.
        </p>
        <p>
          The depth of JRNY integration differs meaningfully between the
          two models. On the M7's larger, more capable console, JRNY can
          drive fully guided, auto-adjusting resistance workouts in real
          time. On the M5's more basic LCD display, JRNY compatibility is
          more limited, functioning primarily as a supplementary tracking
          layer rather than a fully integrated coaching experience. If
          JRNY's guided coaching is a major part of why you're interested
          in the Max Trainer line, that's a real point in favor of the M7
          specifically.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-competitors" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Max Trainer vs. a Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          Bowflex's own treadmills compete head-on with the other brands —
          our <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/">Bowflex T6 review</a> sets the T6
          against the Sole F63 and the T9. For the Max Trainer, though, the
          more useful comparison is between the format and a treadmill.
          Against a
          budget-friendly folding treadmill like the
          <strong>XTERRA TRX3500</strong>, covered in our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/">folding treadmill guide</a>,
          the Max Trainer line trades the ability to run at all for a
          meaningfully smaller footprint, quieter operation, and lower
          joint impact. Neither is objectively better — they answer
          different questions about what kind of cardio you actually want.
        </p>
        <p>
          For readers comparing connected fitness across brands, JRNY sits closest in spirit
          to NordicTrack's iFit — both are optional, subscription-based
          coaching layers on top of hardware that functions without them.
          See our <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/">NordicTrack Commercial 1750 review</a>
          for a look at how that model plays out on an actual treadmill.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-black text-amber-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v3.75m0 3.75h.007v.008H12v-.008zM21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>
            Buying Tip
          </p>
          <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            If you're genuinely torn between a Max Trainer and a treadmill,
            try to test both motion patterns in person before buying —
            many big-box fitness retailers carry both. The difference in
            feel is significant enough that a five-minute in-store trial
            can save you from a purchase you end up not using.
          </p>
        </div>
        </div>

        <div id="specific-needs" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Bowflex for Specific Situations</h2>
        <p>
          A few reader situations come up often enough that they're worth
          addressing directly. For anyone with joint sensitivity — knees,
          hips, or lower back — who still wants an intense cardio option,
          either Max Trainer model is a genuinely strong fit, delivering a
          real cardio workout with meaningfully less impact than running.
          For apartment dwellers specifically worried about noise or
          floor space, the Max Trainer's compact, quiet, quick-folding
          design is a real practical advantage over any treadmill, folding
          or otherwise.
        </p>
        <p>
          For households that already own a treadmill and are considering
          adding a Max Trainer as a second machine, the two genuinely
          complement each other rather than duplicating capability —
          longer, easier-paced sessions on the treadmill, short and
          intense interval days on the Max Trainer, spreading joint impact
          across two different movement patterns. And for anyone whose
          primary goal specifically includes running — training for a
          race, maintaining running mechanics, or simply preferring the
          feel of it — no Bowflex product in the current lineup can
          deliver that, and an actual treadmill from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-for-home-reviews/">home treadmill guide</a>
          is the right purchase.
        </p>

        <p>
          It's also worth addressing resale and long-term ownership value
          briefly, since it's a question we get often about the Max
          Trainer line specifically. Because Bowflex is such a widely
          recognized name in home fitness, both Max Trainer models tend to
          hold resale value reasonably well compared to lesser-known
          compact ellipticals, provided the machine has been kept in good
          working condition. That's a smaller factor than day-to-day fit
          for your training goals, but worth knowing if you're weighing
          the total cost of ownership over several years rather than just
          the upfront price.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Which Bowflex Max Trainer Should You Buy?</h2>
        <p>
          If you're confident you want serious, progressive interval
          training and expect to keep pushing intensity over months and
          years, the <strong>Max Trainer M7</strong> is worth its higher
          price for the extra resistance headroom and deeper JRNY
          integration. If you're newer to this style of training, working
          with a tighter budget, or simply want to confirm the Max Trainer
          format works for you before spending more, the
          <strong>Max Trainer M5</strong> is the smarter, lower-risk
          starting point. Either way, remember the more important decision
          happens before you compare these two models: confirming that
          low-impact interval cardio, not running, is actually what you
          want.
        </p>

        <p>
          One more situation worth addressing directly: readers coming from
          a physical therapy or injury-recovery background specifically
          looking for gentler cardio options. While we're not qualified to
          give medical advice and always recommend checking with a doctor
          or physical therapist before starting any new equipment following
          an injury, the Max Trainer's low-impact motion is frequently
          mentioned in fitness and rehab communities as a reasonable
          reintroduction to cardio training after knee or hip issues that
          make running uncomfortable. That's a meaningfully different use
          case than the HIIT-focused marketing Bowflex leads with, but it's
          a genuine reason some readers land on this page in the first
          place, and it's worth naming explicitly.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Warranty and Customer Support</h2>
        <p>
          Both Max Trainer models carry a 5-year frame warranty, reflecting
          reasonable confidence in the shared structural components across
          the lineup. Parts and electronics coverage differs by model — the
          M7 offers 2 years of parts coverage versus the M5's 1 year, with
          1-year electronics and labor coverage on both. These terms are
          in line with what's typical for compact home cardio equipment in
          this price range, though shorter than the lifetime frame
          warranties offered by commercial-pedigree treadmill brands like
          Life Fitness or Matrix Fitness.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since the 2024 sale, Bowflex support sits within Johnson Health
          Tech, which also supports the Horizon and Matrix lines. Warranty
          terms are set per machine, so check the current terms for the
          exact model you buy. As with
          any equipment purchase, registering your machine promptly and
          keeping your model and serial numbers accessible will speed up
          any future warranty claim.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's worth budgeting for routine maintenance regardless of
          warranty terms. Periodic tightening of the pedal arm pivot bolts
          and keeping the resistance mechanism free of dust are the two
          maintenance items that come up most often in owner feedback
          across both Max Trainer models, and staying on top of them is
          the single biggest factor in avoiding premature wear.
        </p>

        <p>
          Delivery and assembly logistics round out the practical
          ownership considerations worth knowing in advance. Both Max
          Trainer models are considerably lighter and easier to assemble
          solo than any treadmill we review, which means owners rarely
          report the delivery-access headaches that occasionally come up
          with larger, heavier equipment. That's a real, if underrated,
          part of the total ownership experience — no need to coordinate a
          second person for lifting a heavy upright frame, and no need to
          plan a delivery route through a tight stairwell or hallway the
          way you might for a full-size treadmill or a fixed-frame
          commercial machine.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="complaints" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Common Owner Complaints (and How to Avoid Them)</h2>
        <p>
          The single most common complaint tied to the Bowflex brand
          overall isn't about the Max Trainer's performance — it's
          confusion from shoppers expecting a treadmill to exist in the
          lineup, exactly the misunderstanding this guide opened by
          addressing. Going in with accurate expectations about what
          Bowflex actually sells heads off most of that frustration before
          it happens.
        </p>
        <p>
          Among actual Max Trainer owners, the most frequently reported
          issue is the motion pattern taking longer than expected to feel
          natural, particularly for users with no prior elliptical
          experience. This isn't a defect, but it's worth knowing going in
          — most testers and owners report the movement feeling
          comfortable within two to three sessions, so it's worth pushing
          through the initial adjustment period before judging the machine
          on that basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          A smaller but recurring theme involves JRNY subscription value
          perception, with some owners feeling the ongoing cost isn't
          justified given the machine's fully functional built-in
          programs. This mirrors a pattern we see across nearly every
          connected fitness ecosystem, treadmill or otherwise: the
          subscription genuinely adds value for users who want guided,
          varied coaching content, and is a real added cost for users who
          are happy following their own structure.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Bowflex make treadmills?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Yes. Bowflex currently sells three folding treadmills: the T6, the T9 and the T16. None of them needs a subscription to work, and each can connect to the JRNY app and to other training apps, which carry their own subscriptions. Bowflex is also known for the Max Trainer, a low-impact stepper-elliptical hybrid that is not a treadmill. Our Bowflex T6 review covers the entry model in detail.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Bowflex?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Johnson Health Tech, which also owns Horizon Fitness and Matrix Fitness. Bowflex was previously the flagship brand of Nautilus Inc, which renamed itself BowFlex Inc in 2023, filed for Chapter 11 in March 2024 and sold the BowFlex, Schwinn and JRNY brands to Johnson Health Tech in a sale that closed in April 2024.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the JRNY app?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              JRNY is Bowflex's coaching and workout app, offering guided,
              auto-adjusting workouts, progress tracking, and goal-setting
              features across compatible Bowflex equipment including the
              Max Trainer line. It's conceptually similar to iFit on
              NordicTrack treadmills, and it's an optional subscription
              rather than a requirement for using the equipment.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Bowflex Max Trainer should I buy?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              The Max Trainer M7 is the better choice for anyone who
              expects to progress into high-intensity training, thanks to
              its 14 resistance levels and deeper JRNY integration. The Max
              Trainer M5 is the smarter starting point for beginners or
              budget-conscious buyers who want to try the format before
              spending more.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Bowflex Max Trainer a good alternative to a treadmill?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              It depends entirely on your goal. If you want lower-impact
              cardio and don't need to run, the Max Trainer line is a
              legitimate and in some ways superior alternative. If running
              specifically is your goal, no Max Trainer can replace a
              treadmill, and you should look at an actual treadmill
              instead.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Bowflex still make home gyms and strength equipment?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Yes. Bowflex's original and still-current core business is
              home strength equipment, including its signature
              resistance-rod home gym machines and adjustable dumbbells.
              The Max Trainer cardio line is a newer addition to that
              lineup rather than a replacement for it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Common Mistakes -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buyers Make</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most people arriving at this page are shopping for a treadmill, and the mistakes follow from that mismatch.</p>
        <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Bowflex sells a treadmill</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It does not currently. The Max Trainer line is a low-impact hybrid trainer with no running belt. If you want to walk or run indoors, you need a different brand.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Max Trainer as a treadmill substitute</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The stride is not a walking gait. For low-impact cardio it works well; for training that transfers to outdoor running it does not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading JRNY as included content</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The guided programming is a subscription. The machines function without it, but the experience shown in the marketing does not.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing prices without comparing categories</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An M7 costs what a capable treadmill costs. Decide which machine you actually want before letting the price comparison decide for you.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
